{% extends "pages/_docs_guide.jinja" %}
{% import "components.jinja" as ui %}
{% import "_api.jinja" as api %}
{% from "data/docs.jinja" import docs_tested %}
{% set guide_slug = 'observe' %}
{% block guide_body %}
<section aria-labelledby="channels">
  <h2 id="channels">Choose a channel</h2>
  <p>
    Cuprum reports a run through several channels, and each answers a
    different question. Pick by the question, and keep every callback
    bounded:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lifecycle events.</strong> {{ api.ref('observe') }} delivers an {{ api.ref('ExecEvent') }} as a command plans, starts, writes, exits, or times out. Correlate by <code>exec_id</code>, not <code>pid</code>, which the operating system recycles.</li>
    <li><strong>Lines.</strong> <code>on_line</code> or {{ api.ref('SafeCmd.lines') }}, in <a href="/cuprum/docs/guides/output/">Control output</a>, for progress as it happens.</li>
    <li><strong>A heartbeat.</strong> <code>idle_after</code> says that no monitored output arrived; it says nothing about whether the child is stuck.</li>
    <li><strong>Stream operations.</strong> One aggregate event per completed drain or pipeline transfer, for throughput.</li>
    <li><strong>The Rust pump.</strong> Whether each pipeline hop used the native pump or fell back to Python; a decline is a routing decision, not a failure. See the <a href="/cuprum/internals/rust-extension/">Rust extension</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="telemetry">
  <h2 id="telemetry">Log, measure, and trace runs</h2>
  <p>
    The adapters turn lifecycle events into structured log records
    ({{ api.ref('cuprum.adapters.logging_adapter', 'logging_adapter') }}),
    metrics ({{ api.ref('cuprum.adapters.metrics_adapter', 'metrics_adapter') }}),
    and trace spans ({{ api.ref('cuprum.adapters.tracing_adapter', 'tracing_adapter') }}).
    Each is an observe hook. <code>InMemoryMetrics</code> and
    <code>InMemoryTracer</code> are reference backends; production code
    implements the <code>MetricsCollector</code> protocol, or the
    <code>Tracer</code> and <code>Span</code> protocols, over its own
    telemetry library.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('telemetry.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import logging
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh
from cuprum.adapters.logging_adapter import structured_logging_hook
from cuprum.adapters.metrics_adapter import InMemoryMetrics, MetricsHook
from cuprum.adapters.tracing_adapter import InMemoryTracer, TracingHook

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="telemetry")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
metrics = InMemoryMetrics()
tracer = InMemoryTracer()
logger = logging.getLogger("myapp.telemetry")

with (
    sh.observe(structured_logging_hook(logger=logger)),
    sh.observe(MetricsHook(metrics)),
    sh.observe(TracingHook(tracer)),
):
    python("-c", "print('traced')").run_sync()

assert metrics.counters["cuprum_executions_total"] == 1
assert [span.name for span in tracer.spans] and tracer.spans[0].ended
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    The structured logging adapter records <code>argv</code> verbatim, so a
    secret passed as an argument reaches the log: pass secrets through the
    environment or a file. Never turn untrusted arguments into metric
    labels.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="streams">
  <h2 id="streams">Measure stream throughput</h2>
  <p>
    {{ api.ref('cuprum.stream_observation', 'observe_stream_operation') }}
    registers a hook for the pure-Python stream paths. It receives one event
    per completed drain or transfer, never one per read, with the bytes
    moved, the number of reads, and the duration.
    {{ api.ref('cuprum.adapters.stream_metrics', 'stream_operation_metrics_hook') }}
    turns those into counters and a histogram whose only labels are the
    closed <code>operation</code> and <code>outcome</code> values. Observer
    failures are logged and suppressed, so observing never changes a run.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('stream_metrics.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh
from cuprum.adapters.metrics_adapter import InMemoryMetrics
from cuprum.adapters.stream_metrics import stream_operation_metrics_hook
from cuprum.stream_observation import observe_stream_operation

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="stream-metrics")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
metrics = InMemoryMetrics()
with observe_stream_operation(stream_operation_metrics_hook(metrics)):
    python("-c", "print('hello')").run_sync()
assert metrics.counters["cuprum_stream_operation_bytes_total"] > 0
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="actions">
  <h2 id="actions">Present output in GitHub Actions</h2>
  <p>
    On GitHub Actions, Cuprum can frame a run's echoed output in a
    collapsible log group and turn a failed run into an
    <code>::error::</code> annotation. Both are presentation only: capture,
    exit codes, and the result are unchanged. The framing activates only when
    the parent process runs on GitHub Actions (<code>GITHUB_ACTIONS</code> is
    <code>true</code>); elsewhere a run behaves as if it were absent.
  </p>
  <h3>Group and annotate flags</h3>
  <p>
    <code>group</code> and <code>annotate_failure</code> on
    {{ api.ref('RunOutputOptions') }} are the shorthand for the common case.
    They build a <code>GitHubActionsSink</code> and store it as the options'
    sink, so shared options can carry them safely:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('ci_flags.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import dataclasses
import io
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, RunOutputOptions, sh
from cuprum.sinks import GitHubActionsSink

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="ci-flags")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)

shared = RunOutputOptions(echo=True, group=True, annotate_failure=True)
assert isinstance(shared.sink, GitHubActionsSink)
result = python("-c", "print('building')").run_sync(output=shared)
assert result.ok and result.stdout == "building\n"

# An explicit sink wins, so shared flagged options never displace it.
explicit = GitHubActionsSink(io.StringIO(), force=True)
assert dataclasses.replace(shared, sink=explicit).sink is explicit

try:
    RunOutputOptions(group="yes")
except ValueError:
    pass
else:
    raise AssertionError("a non-bool flag should be rejected")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    The flags are independent, and both default to <code>False</code>.
    <code>group=True</code> frames the run in a <code>::group::</code> and
    <code>::endgroup::</code> pair and protects echoed output with a
    stop-commands lease, so child output cannot inject workflow commands; a
    pipeline receives one group for the whole run.
    <code>annotate_failure=True</code> writes one <code>::error::</code>
    annotation when the run ends in a non-zero exit, a timeout, or an error;
    on its own it frames no group and takes no lease.
  </p>
  <p>
    Workflow commands go to the parent's stderr. An explicit
    <code>sink=</code> takes precedence and makes both flags no-ops, which is
    what makes them safe in shared options; pass it on a new
    <code>RunOutputOptions</code> or through
    <code>dataclasses.replace(shared, sink=...)</code>. The flags carry no
    <code>force</code>, and anything but a <code>bool</code> raises
    <code>ValueError</code>. Run grouped commands one after another when they
    share the parent's stderr, or their frames can interleave.
  </p>
  <h3>Configure the sink directly</h3>
  <p>
    Use {{ api.ref('cuprum.sinks', 'GitHubActionsSink') }} for anything the
    flags do not cover: a custom <code>title</code>, another
    <code>destination</code>, <code>force=True</code> to reproduce the
    framing locally, or <code>emit_group=False</code> and
    <code>emit_annotation=False</code> to switch off either half of the
    frame.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('actions_sink.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import io
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, RunOutputOptions, sh
from cuprum.sinks import GitHubActionsSink

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="ci")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
workflow_log = io.StringIO()
sink = GitHubActionsSink(workflow_log, title="Build", force=True)
result = python("-c", "print('building')").run_sync(
    output=RunOutputOptions(echo=True, sink=sink),
)
assert result.ok and result.stdout == "building\n"
assert workflow_log.getvalue().startswith("::group::Build")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
</section>
{% endblock %}
